<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Knowledge graph foundation models (KGFMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geng et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Galkin et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cui et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>
aim to infer missing links over novel knowledge graphs (KGs) that are not part of the training graphs or domains. This task requires generalization to <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">both</em> unseen nodes and unseen relation types. To achieve this, KGFMs rely on learning <em id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">node and relation invariants</em>: structural properties of nodes and relations that are transferable across KGs even when their relational vocabularies differ. This inductive bias is formalized by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al. (2023)</a></cite> as double-equivariance — equivariance under permutations of both entities and relations — and used as a core principle in the design of KGFMs in the literature.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:35%;"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S1.F1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="183.08" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 185.24 183.08" width="185.24"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,183.08) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(94.9,0) translate(0,91.08)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke-width="0.5pt"><path d="M 3.72 0 C 3.72 2.05 2.05 3.72 0 3.72 C -2.05 3.72 -3.72 2.05 -3.72 0 C -3.72 -2.05 -2.05 -3.72 0 -3.72 C 2.05 -3.72 3.72 -2.05 3.72 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.5pt" transform="matrix(0.95 0.0 0.0 0.95 -12.91 -26.01)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.96em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="27.17"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S1.F1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Luke}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖫𝗎𝗄𝖾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Luke}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke-width="0.5pt"><path d="M 3.72 56.1 C 3.72 58.16 2.05 59.82 0 59.82 C -2.05 59.82 -3.72 58.16 -3.72 56.1 C -3.72 54.05 -2.05 52.38 0 52.38 C 2.05 52.38 3.72 54.05 3.72 56.1 Z M 0 56.1"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.5pt" transform="matrix(0.95 0.0 0.0 0.95 -13.86 67.3)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.11em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="29.17"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject