<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Overall pipeline of
<span id="S4.F2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Flock</span>. In each updating step,
<span id="S4.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Flock</span> samples random walks on the KG, anonymizes the encountered nodes and relations via a recording protocol, and feeds the sequences in a sequence processor to compute node and relation representations. A consensus protocol then pools them back to the original KG’s nodes and relations.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Flock</span>, a KGFM respecting probabilistic node-relation invariance.
<span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Flock</span> is a randomized function <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\theta}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\theta}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> which takes as input a KG <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E,R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E,R)</annotation></semantics></math> and a link prediction query <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.
We consider two types of queries: <span id="S4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">entity prediction</span> <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=(h,r,?)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>?</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=(h,r,?)</annotation></semantics></math> and <span id="S4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">relation prediction</span> <math id="S4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=(h,?,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>?</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=(h,?,t)</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Flock</span> outputs a random variable <math id="S4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\bf y}}\sim X_{\theta}(G,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\bf y}}\sim X_{\theta}(G,q)</annotation></semantics></math> which is suited for the task at hand.
For entity prediction, it outputs <math id="S4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\bf y}}:V\to[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\bf y}}:V\to[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> such that a potential link <math id="S4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(h,r,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(h,r,t)</annotation></semantics></math> can be evaluated by <math id="S4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\bf y}}(t)\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\bf y}}(t)\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
For relation prediction, it outputs <math id="S4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\bf y}}:R\to[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\bf y}}:R\to[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> such that a link <math id="S4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(h,r,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(h,r,t)</annotation></semantics></math> can be evaluated by <math id="S4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\bf y}}(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\bf y}}(r)</annotation></semantics></math>.
In practice, we often ensemble repeated predictions <math id="S4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bf y}_{1},...,\hat{\bf y}_{P}\sim X_{\theta}(G,q),P\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>P</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bf y}_{1},...,\hat{\bf y}_{P}\sim X_{\theta}(G,q),P\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> via averaging.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We describe the architecture of <span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Flock</span> in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Flock ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ Flock: A Knowledge Graph Foundation Model via Learning on Random Walks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> focused on four main components, and then analyze its theoretical properties in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Theoretical properties of Flock ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ Flock: A Knowledge Graph Foundation Model via Learning on Random Walks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, showing universality and probabilistic equivariance.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Flock</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Internally, <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Flock</span> has two lookup tables of hidden states, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf v}:V\to\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf v}:V\to\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> for entities and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf r}:R\to\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐫</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf r}:R\to\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> for relations, respectively.
At each forward pass, it starts from trained initializations of these states <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf v}^{(0)}(\cdot)\coloneqq{\bf v}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf v}^{(0)}(\cdot)\coloneqq{\bf v}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf r}^{(0)}(\cdot)\coloneqq{\bf r}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf r}^{(0)}(\cdot)\coloneqq{\bf r}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and updates them iteratively <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf v}^{(i)},{\bf r}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf v}^{(i)},{\bf r}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leq I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leq I</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each update is done residually using a randomized function <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\theta_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\theta_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\bf v}^{(i+1)}\coloneqq{\bf v}^{(i)}+\Delta{\bf v},\quad{\bf r}^{(i+1)}\coloneqq{\bf r}^{(i)}+\Delta{\bf r},\quad(\Delta{\bf v},\Delta{\bf r})\sim{\rm update}_{\theta_{i}}({\bf v}^{(i)},{\bf r}^{(i)})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐫</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐫</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>update</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bf v}^{(i+1)}\coloneqq{\bf v}^{(i)}+\Delta{\bf v},\quad{\bf r}^{(i+1)}\coloneqq{\bf r}^{(i)}+\Delta{\bf r},\quad(\Delta{\bf v},\Delta{\bf r})\sim{\rm update}_{\theta_{i}}({\bf v}^{(i)},{\bf r}^{(i)}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The final hidden states <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf v}^{(I)}:V\to\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf v}^{(I)}:V\to\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf r}^{(I)}:R\to\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf r}^{(I)}:R\to\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> are then processed by a binary classifier <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm head}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>head</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm head}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> to produce the output <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bf y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bf y}</annotation></semantics></math> which is <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\to[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\to[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\to[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\to[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> depending on task.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We now describe the randomized <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm update}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>update</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm update}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We drop <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> for brevity.
It consists of four components:</p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:2em;">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Random walk algorithm</span> produces <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> random walks <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{1},...,\eta_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{1},...,\eta_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> of length <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> on the input KG.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Recording protocol</span> <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w:\eta_{j}\mapsto{\bf z}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w:\eta_{j}\mapsto{\bf z}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> transforms each walk into a graph-agnostic sequence.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sequence processor</span> <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}:{\bf z}_{j}\mapsto{\bf h}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}:{\bf z}_{j}\mapsto{\bf h}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> processes each sequence independently, outputting features.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Consensus protocol</span> <math id="S4.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="c:({\bf h}_{1:N},\eta_{1:N})\mapsto(\Delta{\bf v},\Delta{\bf r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c:({\bf h}_{1:N},\eta_{1:N})\mapsto(\Delta{\bf v},\Delta{\bf r})</annotation></semantics></math> collects features of all walks and decides state updates for each entity and relation type.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">An overview is presented in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ Flock: A Knowledge Graph Foundation Model via Learning on Random Walks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
We note that <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> are all deterministic, and the random walk is the only source of stochasticity.
We now discuss the design choice for each.
For the ease of exposition, we explain for entity prediction tasks <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=(h,r,?)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>?</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=(h,r,?)</annotation></semantics></math>, but relation prediction is similar.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Random walks.</span>
In <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Flock</span>, random walks are central in two ways: they rewrite the connectivity of nodes and relations as sequences, and support generalization via probabilistic equivariance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, the random walk algorithm produces <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> random walks <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{1},...,\eta_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{1},...,\eta_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> of length <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> on KG <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each random walk <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> is a chain of random variables, written as:n as:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\eta=v_{0}\xrightarrow{r_{1}}v_{1}\xrightarrow{r_{2}}\cdots\xrightarrow{r_{l}}v_{l},\quad v_{s}\in V,r_{s}\in R,(v_{s-1},r_{s},v_{s})\in E," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub></mover><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.1389em">→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">2</mn></msub></mover><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0.1389em">→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">l</mi></msub></mover><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\eta=v_{0}\xrightarrow{r_{1}}v_{1}\xrightarrow{r_{2}}\cdots\xrightarrow{r_{l}}v_{l},\quad v_{s}\in V,r_{s}\in R,(v_{s-1},r_{s},v_{s})\in E,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where the underlying transition mechanism and <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> are hyperparameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To support probabilistic equivariance, we ask the walk algorithm to be invariant in probability.
We say <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> is invariant in probability if for any <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\simeq H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>≃</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\simeq H</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{K}_{n,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕂</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{K}_{n,m}</annotation></semantics></math> with isomorphism <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pi,\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi,\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi(v_{0})\xrightarrow{\phi(r_{1})}\pi(v_{1})\xrightarrow{\phi(r_{2})}\cdots\xrightarrow{\phi(r_{l})}\pi(v_{l})\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\mathclap{\text{{\mbox{$d$}}}}}}{{=}}u_{0}\xrightarrow{s_{1}}u_{1}\xrightarrow{s_{2}}\cdots\xrightarrow{s_{l}}u_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mover><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.1389em">→</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">2</mn></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0.1389em">→</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">l</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mover><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo>=</mo><mpadded style="width:0pt;" lspace="-2.1pt" width="0pt"><mi>d</mi></mpadded></mover><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub></mover><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.1389em">→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">2</mn></msub></mover><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0.1389em">→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">l</mi></msub></mover><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi(v_{0})\xrightarrow{\phi(r_{1})}\pi(v_{1})\xrightarrow{\phi(r_{2})}\cdots\xrightarrow{\phi(r_{l})}\pi(v_{l})\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\mathclap{\text{{\mbox{$d$}}}}}}{{=}}u_{0}\xrightarrow{s_{1}}u_{1}\xrightarrow{s_{2}}\cdots\xrightarrow{s_{l}}u_{l}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{0}\xrightarrow{r_{1}}\cdots\xrightarrow{r_{l}}v_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.1389em">→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub></mover><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0.1389em">→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">l</mi></msub></mover><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{0}\xrightarrow{r_{1}}\cdots\xrightarrow{r_{l}}v_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{0}\xrightarrow{s_{1}}\cdots\xrightarrow{s_{l}}u_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.1389em">→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub></mover><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0.1389em">→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">l</mi></msub></mover><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{0}\xrightarrow{s_{1}}\cdots\xrightarrow{s_{l}}u_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> follow the distributions of <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta(G,l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta(G,l)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta(H,l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta(H,l)</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
In such case, the isomorphism <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pi,\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi,\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> yields a natural translation from walks in <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In <span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Flock</span>, we use a simple random walk algorithm, which we show to be invariant in probability, while working robustly in practice.
Specifically, given a base walk count <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, for entity prediction queries <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(h,r,?)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>?</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(h,r,?)</annotation></semantics></math>, we use <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3n</annotation></semantics></math> walks and choose three types of start locations.
We start <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> walks at query node <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> by fixing <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{0}=h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{0}=h</annotation></semantics></math>, start <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> walks by choosing a random relation <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, then a random edge <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v,s,u)\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v,s,u)\in E</annotation></semantics></math>, and then fixing the first step as <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\xrightarrow{s}u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑠</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\xrightarrow{s}u</annotation></semantics></math>, and start the rest of <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> walks at random nodes in <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>. For relation prediction queries <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(h,?,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>?</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(h,?,t)</annotation></semantics></math>, we additionally start <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> walks at the tail node <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> by fixing <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{0}=t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{0}=t</annotation></semantics></math>, sampling a total of <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4n</annotation></semantics></math> walks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">For the transition mechanism, we use uniform walks with non-backtracking, with minor modifications to handle directed multi-edges of KGs.
Despite the simplicity, we find that this choice works well in practice, consistent with findings of prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tönshoff et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We lastly discuss how to choose the base walk count <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.
While this is a fixed hyperparameter <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\rm train}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>train</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\rm train}</annotation></semantics></math> at pretraining, we find that scaling it adaptively to input KG at test-time benefits size generalization. We thus propose <em id="S4.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">test-time adaptation of walk counts</em>, and use:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n=n_{\rm train}\times{\rm harmonic}\ {\rm mean}\left(\frac{|V|}{|V|_{\rm train}},\frac{|E|}{|E|_{\rm train}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>train</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>harmonic</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>mean</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>train</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>train</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n=n_{\rm train}\times{\rm harmonic}\ {\rm mean}\left(\frac{|V|}{|V|_{\rm train}},\frac{|E|}{|E|_{\rm train}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p8.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V|_{\rm train},|E|_{\rm train}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>train</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>train</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V|_{\rm train},|E|_{\rm train}</annotation></semantics></math> are average numbers of nodes and edges in pretraining KGs, respectively.
Intuitively, this scales <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> proportionally to the sizes of test KGs relative to pretraining.
In practice, we clamp <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> to the nearest power of <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> in an interval to keep GPU memory usage in a range.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Recording protocol.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While random walks provide a basis for invariant sequence representations of KGs, two issues remain: (1) They reveal nodes <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and relations <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> specific to each KG which obstructs transferability to unseen KGs. (2) They do not offer a way to condition on current states of entities <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf v}</annotation></semantics></math>, relations <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf r}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the query <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=(h,r,?)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>?</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=(h,r,?)</annotation></semantics></math> as often done in KGFMs via the labeling trick.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The recording protocol <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w:\eta_{j}\mapsto{\bf z}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w:\eta_{j}\mapsto{\bf z}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> resolves this by transforming each walk into a <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">graph-agnostic</em> sequence that only leaves structural information.
Motivated by prior works on node anonymization for invariance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang &amp; Cho, 2024</a>)</cite>, we propose an extension called node-relation anonymization: reserve separate namespaces for nodes and relations, respectively, and assign unique names in the order of discovery.
For example, with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,2,3,..." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,2,3,...</annotation></semantics></math> for nodes and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha,\beta,..." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,\beta,...</annotation></semantics></math> for relations:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\eta=v_{0}\xrightarrow{r_{1}}v_{1}\xrightarrow{r_{2}}v_{2}\xrightarrow{r_{1}^{-1}}v_{0}\qquad\mapsto\qquad 1\xrightarrow{\alpha}2\xrightarrow{\beta}3\xrightarrow{\alpha^{-1}}1," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub></mover><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">2</mn></msub></mover><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn><mrow><mo mathsize="0.710em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mover><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mn>1</mn><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝛼</mo></mover><mn>2</mn><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝛽</mo></mover><mn>3</mn><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">α</mi><mrow><mo mathsize="0.710em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></mrow></msup></mover><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\eta=v_{0}\xrightarrow{r_{1}}v_{1}\xrightarrow{r_{2}}v_{2}\xrightarrow{r_{1}^{-1}}v_{0}\qquad\mapsto\qquad 1\xrightarrow{\alpha}2\xrightarrow{\beta}3\xrightarrow{\alpha^{-1}}1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\cdot)^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\cdot)^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> marks direction of a relation.
The protocol additionally employs a simple conditioning on current states <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bf v},{\bf r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bf v},{\bf r})</annotation></semantics></math> and query <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=(h,r,?)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>?</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=(h,r,?)</annotation></semantics></math>, completing the record <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf z}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle w:\eta\mapsto{\bf z}=(1,{\bf v}(v_{0}),{\bf 1}_{h}(v_{0}))\xrightarrow{\alpha,{\bf r}(r_{1}),{\bf 1}_{r}(r_{1})}(2,{\bf v}(v_{1}),{\bf 1}_{h}(v_{1}))\xrightarrow{\beta,{\bf r}(r_{2}),{\bf 1}_{r}(r_{2})}\cdots," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">α</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mn mathsize="0.700em">𝟏</mn><mi mathsize="0.710em">r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.1389em">→</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">β</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">2</mn></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mn mathsize="0.700em">𝟏</mn><mi mathsize="0.710em">r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">2</mn></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle w:\eta\mapsto{\bf z}=(1,{\bf v}(v_{0}),{\bf 1}_{h}(v_{0}))\xrightarrow{\alpha,{\bf r}(r_{1}),{\bf 1}_{r}(r_{1})}(2,{\bf v}(v_{1}),{\bf 1}_{h}(v_{1}))\xrightarrow{\beta,{\bf r}(r_{2}),{\bf 1}_{r}(r_{2})}\cdots,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">with indicator functions <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf 1}_{h}(\cdot),{\bf 1}_{r}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf 1}_{h}(\cdot),{\bf 1}_{r}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
As we will show, the recording protocol keeps node-relation invariance by hiding nodes and relations while leaving their structural roles.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Sequence processor.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Now that the recordings <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf z}</annotation></semantics></math> only encode structural information of KG, we can safely process them with an arbitrary neural network <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}:{\bf z}\mapsto{\bf h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>𝐡</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}:{\bf z}\mapsto{\bf h}</annotation></semantics></math> without the risk of losing invariance.
Since <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf z}</annotation></semantics></math> are sequences, we choose sequence networks to leverage their inductive bias.
Specifically, we use bidirectional GRU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cho et al., 2014</a>)</cite> equipped with RMSNorm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang &amp; Sennrich, 2019</a>)</cite> and SwiGLU feedforward network <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shazeer, 2020</a>)</cite>, which provided robust results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given that <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sequence network, it is convenient to interpret its output <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf h}</annotation></semantics></math> as positionally aligned with each step of the walk <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> or record <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf z}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Specifically, for the example in Equation <a href="#S4.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ Recording protocol. ‣ 4.1 Flock ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ Flock: A Knowledge Graph Foundation Model via Learning on Random Walks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, we obtain:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f_{\theta}:{\bf z}\mapsto{\bf h}=(\Delta{\bf v}_{0},a_{0})\xrightarrow{\Delta{\bf r}_{1},b_{1}}(\Delta{\bf v}_{1},a_{1})\xrightarrow{\Delta{\bf r}_{2},b_{2}}\cdots." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐫</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">b</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub></mrow></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.1389em">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐫</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">2</mn></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">b</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">2</mn></msub></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{\theta}:{\bf z}\mapsto{\bf h}=(\Delta{\bf v}_{0},a_{0})\xrightarrow{\Delta{\bf r}_{1},b_{1}}(\Delta{\bf v}_{1},a_{1})\xrightarrow{\Delta{\bf r}_{2},b_{2}}\cdots.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta{\bf v}_{s},\Delta{\bf r}_{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{h\times d_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta{\bf v}_{s},\Delta{\bf r}_{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{h\times d_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{s},b_{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>h</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{s},b_{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{h}</annotation></semantics></math> are the decoded outputs at each position using linear projections.
Intuitively, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta{\bf v}_{s},\Delta{\bf r}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta{\bf v}_{s},\Delta{\bf r}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> encode proposals of state updates for entities and relations by <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{s},b_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{s},b_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> encode respective confidences of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> for the proposed updates.
This separation is useful due to the localized, pure-structure nature of the recordings <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If a random walk <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> densely visited a cycle-like region and then terminated in a dangling manner, it is natural to assign more confidence to the cycle-like region of the structural encodings <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf h}</annotation></semantics></math>, and less confidence to the dangling region.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Consensus protocol.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After sequence processing, we are left with a handful of state update proposals <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\bf h}_{1:N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf h}_{1:N}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, that are positionally aligned with random walks <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\eta_{1:N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{1:N}</annotation></semantics></math> on KG <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E,R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E,R)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The consensus protocol <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> uses the information to decide final state updates <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta{\bf v}:V\to\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta{\bf v}:V\to\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta{\bf r}:R\to\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐫</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta{\bf r}:R\to\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> can access how each <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta{\bf v}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta{\bf v}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> within <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf h}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf h}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is associated to a node <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{s}\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{s}\in V</annotation></semantics></math> (and how each <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta{\bf r}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta{\bf r}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is associated to a relation <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{s}\in R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{s}\in R</annotation></semantics></math>) through the random walk <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, a simple way to form a consensus is by finding all proposals <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\Delta{\bf v}_{s}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\Delta{\bf v}_{s}\}</annotation></semantics></math> associated to each node <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, and all <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\Delta{\bf r}_{s}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\Delta{\bf r}_{s}\}</annotation></semantics></math> associated to each relation <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, and take averages of these proposals.
The drawback is that uninformative proposals from e.g., dangling regions of walks are not directly suppressed, and can affect the state updates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We can leverage the confidences <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{s},b_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{s},b_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> to alleviate this issue.
For each node <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math> or relation <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in R</annotation></semantics></math>, we first find all respective associated pairs <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(\Delta{\bf v}_{s},a_{s})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(\Delta{\bf v}_{s},a_{s})\}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(\Delta{\bf r}_{s},b_{s})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(\Delta{\bf r}_{s},b_{s})\}</annotation></semantics></math> of proposals and confidences, and compute a multi-head softmax-normalized weighted average:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta{\bf v}(v)\coloneqq\left[\sum\exp(a_{s})\odot\Delta{\bf v}_{s}\right]\oslash\sum\exp(a_{s})\quad\Delta{\bf r}(r)\coloneqq\left[\sum\exp(b_{s})\odot\Delta{\bf r}_{s}\right]\oslash\sum\exp(b_{s})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>⊘</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>⊘</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta{\bf v}(v)\coloneqq\left[\sum\exp(a_{s})\odot\Delta{\bf v}_{s}\right]\oslash\sum\exp(a_{s})\quad\Delta{\bf r}(r)\coloneqq\left[\sum\exp(b_{s})\odot\Delta{\bf r}_{s}\right]\oslash\sum\exp(b_{s}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\odot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\odot</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\oslash" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊘</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\oslash</annotation></semantics></math> are row-wise multiplication and division, respectively.
Intuitively, this normalization induces competition between state update proposals, naturally leading to uninformative proposals being suppressed.
Similar ideas are presented by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Locatello et al. (2020)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Again, we can show that the consensus protocol does not operate in a way specific to particular KGs, and hence retains node-relation equivariance.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Theoretical properties of <span id="S4.SS2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Flock</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Expressivity. </span>
Following the notion of probabilistic expressivity introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abboud et al. (2021)</a></cite>, we say that a <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Flock</span> model <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is a universal approximator of link invariant functions over <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{K}_{n,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕂</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{K}_{n,m}</annotation></semantics></math> if for any link invariant <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:\mathbb{K}_{n,m}\rightarrow(V\times R\times V\rightarrow[0,1])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕂</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:\mathbb{K}_{n,m}\rightarrow(V\times R\times V\rightarrow[0,1])</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon,\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon,\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a choice of the network parameters <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> and the length of the sampled random walks <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>, such that:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}(|\varphi(G)((h,r,t))-X_{\theta}(G,(h,r,?))(t)|&lt;\epsilon)&gt;1-\delta" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo>,</mo><mo>,</mo><mo>,</mo><mo>,</mo><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>?</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}(|\varphi(G)((h,r,t))-X_{\theta}(G,(h,r,?))(t)|&lt;\epsilon)&gt;1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">for all graphs <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E,R)\in\mathbb{K}_{n,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝕂</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E,R)\in\mathbb{K}_{n,m}</annotation></semantics></math> and all links <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(h,r,t)\in V\times R\times V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(h,r,t)\in V\times R\times V</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmglobalcounter1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmglobalcounter1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmglobalcounter1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmglobalcounter1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmglobalcounter1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmglobalcounter1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">With a powerful enough sequence processor <math id="S4.Thmglobalcounter1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <span id="S4.Thmglobalcounter1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Flock</span> framework described above is a universal approximator of link invariant functions over <math id="S4.Thmglobalcounter1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{K}_{n,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕂</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{K}_{n,m}</annotation></semantics></math> for all pairs <math id="S4.Thmglobalcounter1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,m)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Invariance. </span>
Despite the stochastic nature of our framework, beyond randomized node embeddings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abboud et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Flock</span> can be provably guaranteed to satisfy probabilistic invariance:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmglobalcounter2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmglobalcounter2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmglobalcounter2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmglobalcounter2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmglobalcounter2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmglobalcounter2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose that the walk sampling protocol <math id="S4.Thmglobalcounter2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> is invariant in probability and both the recording protocol <math id="S4.Thmglobalcounter2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> and the consensus protocol <math id="S4.Thmglobalcounter2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> are invariant.
Then, regardless of the choice of the deterministic sequence processor <math id="S4.Thmglobalcounter2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, the corresponding <span id="S4.Thmglobalcounter2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Flock</span> model is invariant in probability.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, the designs of <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Flock</span>’s components provided earlier in this section satisfy the conditions of Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmglobalcounter2" title="Proposition 4.2. ‣ 4.2 Theoretical properties of Flock ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ Flock: A Knowledge Graph Foundation Model via Learning on Random Walks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>.
Therefore, the suggested pipeline is indeed invariant in probability:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmglobalcounter3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmglobalcounter3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmglobalcounter3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmglobalcounter3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmglobalcounter3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmglobalcounter3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Any <span id="S4.Thmglobalcounter3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Flock</span> model with components as outlined in this section, and detailed in <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Methodology - details ‣ Flock: A Knowledge Graph Foundation Model via Learning on Random Walks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> is invariant in probability.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Flock</span> over a wide range of KGs with both entity and relation prediction tasks, aiming to answer the following questions:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:2em;">
<li id="S5.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S5.I1.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q1.</span></span> 
<div id="S5.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Can <span id="S5.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Flock</span> approximate functions that existing KGFMs cannot?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S5.I1.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q2.</span></span> 
<div id="S5.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">How does <span id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Flock</span> generalize to unseen entities and relations compared to existing KGFMs?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S5.I1.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q3.</span></span> 
<div id="S5.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">How does performance scale with the pretraining graph mix and the size of test-time ensemble?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.ix4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S5.I1.ix4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q4.</span></span> 
<div id="S5.I1.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">What is the impact of test-time adaptation of walk counts?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.ix5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S5.I1.ix5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q5.</span></span> 
<div id="S5.I1.ix5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.ix5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">How do current KGFMs perform under noise injection, and how do they compare with <span id="S5.I1.ix5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Flock</span>? (see <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Noise injection over existing KGFMs ‣ Flock: A Knowledge Graph Foundation Model via Learning on Random Walks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>)</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to these experiments, we report the computational complexity of <span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Flock</span> in <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Computational Complexity ‣ Flock: A Knowledge Graph Foundation Model via Learning on Random Walks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> and conduct a detailed scalability analysis in <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Scalability analysis ‣ Flock: A Knowledge Graph Foundation Model via Learning on Random Walks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>. We report ensembled predictions by averaging <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> independent stochastic forward passes to reduce variance. Please refer to the detailed per-dataset choices in <a href="#A7.T22" title="In Training. ‣ Appendix G Further experimental details ‣ Flock: A Knowledge Graph Foundation Model via Learning on Random Walks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Synthetic datasets experiments</h3>

<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:38%;"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.F3.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="134.28" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 166 134.28" width="166"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,134.28) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(83,0) translate(0,67.14)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke-width="0.5pt"><path d="M 3.91 0 C 3.91 2.16 2.16 3.91 0 3.91 C -2.16 3.91 -3.91 2.16 -3.91 0 C -3.91 -2.16 -2.16 -3.91 0 -3.91 C 2.16 -3.91 3.91 -2.16 3.91 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.5pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.24 -15.9)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.47em;--ltx-fo-height:0.43em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="5.96" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 5.96)" width="6.49"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.F3.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke-width="0.5pt"><path d="M 3.91 59.06 C 3.91 61.22 2.16 62.97 0 62.97 C -2.16 62.97 -3.91 61.22 -3.91 59.06 C -3.91 56.89 -2.16 55.14 0 55.14 C 2.16 55.14 3.91 56.89 3.91 59.06 Z M 0 59.06"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke-width="0.5pt"><path d="M -35.46 39.37 C -35.46 41.53 -37.21 43.28 -39.37 43.28 C -41.53 43.28 -43.28 41.53 -43.28 39.37 C -43.28 37.21 -41.53 35.46 -39.37 35.46 C -37.21 35.46 -35.46 37.21 -35.46 39.37 Z M -39.37 39.37"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.5pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -44.97 52.34)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.81em;--ltx-fo-height:0.62em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.15em;font-size:10pt;" height="10.59" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.51)" width="11.2"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.F3.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke-width="0.5pt"><path d="M -35.46 -39.37 C -35.46 -37.21 -37.21 -35.46 -39.37 -35.46 C -41.53 -35.46 -43.28 -37.21 -43.28 -39.37 C -43.28 -41.53 -41.53 -43.28 -39.37 -43.28 C -37.21 -43.28 -35.46 -41.53 -35.46 -39.37 Z M -39.37 -39.37"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.5pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -44.97 -58.77)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.81em;--ltx-fo-height:0.62em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.15em;font-size:10pt;" height="10.59" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.51)" width="11.2"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.F3.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke-width="0.5pt"><path d="M -74.83 0 C -74.83 2.16 -76.58 3.91 -78.74 3.91 C -80.9 3.91 -82.65 2.16 -82.65 0 C -82.65 -2.16 -80.9 -3.91 -78.74 -3.91 C -76.58 -3.91 -74.83 -2.16 -74.83 0 Z M -78.74 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke-width="0.5pt"><path d="M 43.28 39.37 C 43.28 41.53 41.53 43.28 39.37 43.28 C 37.21 43.28 35.46 41.53 35.46 39.37 C 35.46 37.21 37.21 35.46 39.37 35.46 C 41.53 35.46 43.28 37.21 43.28 39.37 Z M 39.37 39.37"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke-width="0.5pt"><path d="M 43.28 -39.37 C 43.28 -37.21 41.53 -35.46 39.37 -35.46 C 37.21 -35.46 35.46 -37.21 35.46 -39.37 C 35.46 -41.53 37.21 -43.28 39.37 -43.28 C 41.53 -43.28 43.28 -41.53 43.28 -39.37 Z M 39.37 -39.37"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke-width="0.5pt"><path d="M 82.65 0 C 82.65 2.16 80.9 3.91 78.74 3.91 C 76.58 3.91 74.83 2.16 74.83 0 C 74.83 -2.16 76.58 -3.91 78.74 -3.91 C 80.9 -3.91 82.65 -2.16 82.65 0 Z M 78.74 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke-width="1.0pt"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#50BE50;--ltx-fill-color:#50BE50;" fill="#50BE50" stroke="#50BE50"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 8.41 L 0 43.89"></path><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#50BE50;" color="#50BE50" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(0.0 1.0 -1.0 0.0 0 40.27)"><path d="M 8.53 0 L 2.23 2.34 L 3.97 0 L 2.23 -2.34 Z"></path></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#5050BE;--ltx-fill-color:#5050BE;" fill="#5050BE" stroke="#5050BE"><path style="fill:none" d="M -5.95 5.95 L -28.65 28.65"></path><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#5050BE;" color="#5050BE" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(-0.7071 0.7071 -0.7071 -0.7071 -26.08 26.08)"><path d="M 8.53 0 L 2.23 2.34 L 3.97 0 L 2.23 -2.34 Z"></path></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#BE5050;--ltx-fill-color:#BE5050;" fill="#BE5050" stroke="#BE5050"><path style="fill:none" d="M -5.95 -5.95 L -28.65 -28.65"></path><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#BE5050;" color="#BE5050" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(-0.7071 -0.7071 0.7071 -0.7071 -26.08 -26.08)"><path d="M 8.53 0 L 2.23 2.34 L 3.97 0 L 2.23 -2.34 Z"></path></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#BE5050;--ltx-fill-color:#BE5050;" fill="#BE5050" stroke="#BE5050"><path style="fill:none" d="M -45.32 33.42 L -68.02 10.72"></path><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#BE5050;" color="#BE5050" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(-0.7071 -0.7071 0.7071 -0.7071 -65.45 13.29)"><path d="M 8.53 0 L 2.23 2.34 L 3.97 0 L 2.23 -2.34 Z"></path></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#BE5050;--ltx-fill-color:#BE5050;" fill="#BE5050" stroke="#BE5050"><path style="fill:none" d="M -45.32 -33.42 L -68.02 -10.72"></path><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#BE5050;" color="#BE5050" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(-0.7071 0.7071 -0.7071 -0.7071 -65.45 -13.29)"><path d="M 8.53 0 L 2.23 2.34 L 3.97 0 L 2.23 -2.34 Z"></path></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#BE5050;--ltx-fill-color:#BE5050;" fill="#BE5050" stroke="#BE5050"><path style="fill:none" d="M 5.95 5.95 L 28.65 28.65"></path><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#BE5050;" color="#BE5050" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(0.7071 0.7071 -0.7071 0.7071 26.08 26.08)"><path d="M 8.53 0 L 2.23 2.34 L 3.97 0 L 2.23 -2.34 Z"></path></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#5050BE;--ltx-fill-color:#5050BE;" fill="#5050BE" stroke="#5050BE"><path style="fill:none" d="M 5.95 -5.95 L 28.65 -28.65"></path><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#5050BE;" color="#5050BE" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(0.7071 -0.7071 0.7071 0.7071 26.08 -26.08)"><path d="M 8.53 0 L 2.23 2.34 L 3.97 0 L 2.23 -2.34 Z"></path></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#5050BE;--ltx-fill-color:#5050BE;" fill="#5050BE" stroke="#5050BE"><path style="fill:none" d="M 45.32 33.42 L 68.02 10.72"></path><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#5050BE;" color="#5050BE" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(0.7071 -0.7071 0.7071 0.7071 65.45 13.29)"><path d="M 8.53 0 L 2.23 2.34 L 3.97 0 L 2.23 -2.34 Z"></path></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#5050BE;--ltx-fill-color:#5050BE;" fill="#5050BE" stroke="#5050BE"><path style="fill:none" d="M 45.32 -33.42 L 68.02 -10.72"></path><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#5050BE;" color="#5050BE" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(0.7071 0.7071 -0.7071 0.7071 65.45 -13.29)"><path d="M 8.53 0 L 2.23 2.34 L 3.97 0 L 2.23 -2.34 Z"></path></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#50BE50;--ltx-fill-color:#50BE50;" fill="#50BE50" stroke="#50BE50"><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#50BE50;" color="#50BE50" stroke-dasharray="3.0pt,3.0pt" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -8.28 1.46 C -22.97 4.05 -35.32 16.4 -36.74 24.44"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#50BE50;" color="#50BE50" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(-0.17365 0.98482 -0.98482 -0.17365 -36.11 20.87)"><path d="M 8.53 0 L 2.23 2.34 L 3.97 0 L 2.23 -2.34 Z"></path></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#50BE50;--ltx-fill-color:#50BE50;" fill="#50BE50" stroke="#50BE50"><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#50BE50;" color="#50BE50" stroke-dasharray="3.0pt,3.0pt" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -8.28 -1.46 C -22.97 -4.05 -35.32 -16.4 -36.74 -24.44"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#50BE50;" color="#50BE50" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(-0.17365 -0.98482 0.98482 -0.17365 -36.11 -20.87)"><path d="M 8.53 0 L 2.23 2.34 L 3.97 0 L 2.23 -2.34 Z"></path></g></g></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Example KG from <span id="S5.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Petals</span>. KGFMs with relational invariants must equate blue <math id="S5.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#5050BE;" mathcolor="#5050BE">r</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#5050BE;" mathcolor="#5050BE">1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and red <math id="S5.F3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#BE5050;" mathcolor="#BE5050">r</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#BE5050;" mathcolor="#BE5050">2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, thus predicting the same scores for both dashed queries with <math id="S5.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#409840;" mathcolor="#409840">r</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#409840;" mathcolor="#409840">0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setup.</span>
We construct a synthetic benchmark <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Petals</span> to validate the limitations of KGFMs following the node-relation equivariance (<span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q1</span>).
The <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Petals</span> benchmark contains <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="220" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>220</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">220</annotation></semantics></math> instances, with each instance including the following elements:
1) a KG <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E,R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E,R)</annotation></semantics></math>, which consists of a ‘central’ node <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in V</annotation></semantics></math>, a ‘stem’ <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\subset V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\subset V</annotation></semantics></math> with query relation <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{0}\in R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{0}\in R</annotation></semantics></math>, and multiple cyclic ‘petals’, each ‘colored’ with a different pair of relations in <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\setminus\{r_{0}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\setminus\{r_{0}\}</annotation></semantics></math>,
2) an entity prediction query <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(h,r_{0},?)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>?</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(h,r_{0},?)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in\{s\}\cup T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in\{s\}\cup T</annotation></semantics></math>,
and 3) two candidate targets <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> from the same ‘petal’, located at the same distance from <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>.
An example is shown in <a href="#S5.F3" title="In 5.1 Synthetic datasets experiments ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Flock: A Knowledge Graph Foundation Model via Learning on Random Walks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. See <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Details of the Petals benchmark ‣ Flock: A Knowledge Graph Foundation Model via Learning on Random Walks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for more details.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Petals</span> is designed such that each instance always admits non-trivial automorphisms, meaning that swapping relations occurring in the same ‘petal’ will result in an isomorphic KG.
Consequently, any model computing relation invariants will not be able to distinguish between potential links <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,r_{0},t_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,r_{0},t_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,r_{0},t_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,r_{0},t_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, the samples are constructed so that these links are not isomorphic from the graph perspective, making them distinguishable for general link-invariant functions.
We say a model <em id="S5.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">successfully solves</em> an instance if it can classify <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,r_{0},t_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,r_{0},t_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> as <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">true</span> and <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,r_{0},t_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,r_{0},t_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> as <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">false</span>, and we measure the accuracy in the experiment.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table ltx_align_floatright" style="width:32%;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Accuracy of KGFMs of our <span id="S5.T1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Petals</span> benchmark.</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T1.4" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T1.4.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.4.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model</span></th>
<td id="S5.T1.4.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T1.4.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Petals</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.4.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.4.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T1.4.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Ultra</span></th>
<td id="S5.T1.4.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">50%</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.4.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.4.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><span id="S5.T1.4.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Motif</span>(<math id="S5.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{\text{Path}}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>Path</mtext><mn>3</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{\text{Path}}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>)</th>
<td id="S5.T1.4.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">50%</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.4.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.4.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><span id="S5.T1.4.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Trix</span></th>
<td id="S5.T1.4.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">50%</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.4.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.4.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T1.4.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Flock</span></th>
<td id="S5.T1.4.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T1.4.5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">100%</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We trained <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Ultra</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Galkin et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsc{Motif}({\mathcal{F}}_{\text{Path}}^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Motif</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>Path</mtext><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsc{Motif}({\mathcal{F}}_{\text{Path}}^{3})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Trix</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Flock</span> from scratch and validated them on the training instances.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span>
We present the results in <a href="#S5.T1" title="In 5.1 Synthetic datasets experiments ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Flock: A Knowledge Graph Foundation Model via Learning on Random Walks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
As expected, all existing KGFMs relying on learning deterministic relational invariants fail to distinguish between the candidate target triplets completely, achieving <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50\%</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy due to random guesses.
In contrast, <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Flock</span> succeeds on <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">all</span> considered instances, displaying that, while remaining invariant in probability, it can differentiate between non-isomorphic links, even with isomorphic relations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Entity and relation prediction over knowledge graphs</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setup.</span> We follow the protocol of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Galkin et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024)</a></cite> and pretrain <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Flock</span> on FB15k-237 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Toutanova &amp; Chen, 2015</a>)</cite>, WN18RR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dettmers et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, and CoDEx Medium <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Safavi &amp; Koutra, 2020</a>)</cite>. We then evaluate its zero-shot and finetuned inference performance with the test set of 54 KG datasets (see <a href="#A7.T15" title="In Appendix G Further experimental details ‣ Flock: A Knowledge Graph Foundation Model via Learning on Random Walks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> for details). These KG datasets are extracted from diverse domains across three settings: inductive on nodes and relations (<span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inductive</span> <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e,r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e,r</annotation></semantics></math>), inductive on nodes (<span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inductive</span> <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>), and <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">transductive</span>. Note that these settings differ only during finetuning setup; in zero-shot setup, all entities and relations are unseen.
We choose two state-of-the-art KGFMs <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Ultra</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Galkin et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Trix</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> as baselines, since they are pretrained on the same pretraining graph mix to ensure a fair comparison.
Following conventions in the literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, for each triple <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(h,r,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(h,r,t)</annotation></semantics></math>, we add the corresponding inverse triple <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t,r^{-1},h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t,r^{-1},h)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a fresh relation symbol.
For entity prediction, we report both head and tail results for each triple on all datasets, except for three from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lv et al. (2020)</a></cite> where only tail results are available, following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024)</a></cite>.
For evaluation, we use the filtered ranking protocol <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bordes et al., 2013</a>)</cite>, reporting Mean Reciprocal Rank (MRR) and Hits@10 for entity prediction, and Hits@1 for relation prediction, as some datasets have fewer than <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> relations. Detailed per-dataset results are shown in <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Further experimental details ‣ Flock: A Knowledge Graph Foundation Model via Learning on Random Walks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Entity prediction results.</span>
We present the average entity prediction results in <a href="#S5.T2" title="In 5.2 Entity and relation prediction over knowledge graphs ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Flock: A Knowledge Graph Foundation Model via Learning on Random Walks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (<span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q2</span>).
In the zero-shot setting, <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Flock</span> consistently outperforms <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Ultra</span> and <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Trix</span> on all metrics, demonstrating its strong generalization on KGs over diverse domains. Notably, on <em id="S5.SS2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Metafam</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, a dataset designed to challenge models with conflicting and compositional relational
patterns, <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Flock</span> roughly doubles MRR over <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Ultra</span> and achieves about a 40% MRR gain over <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Trix</span> in zero-shot performance.
These gains align with our hypothesis that probabilistic node–relation equivariance improves expressivity without sacrificing generalization.
In the finetuning setting, we observe a similar pattern: <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Flock</span> maintains a consistent improvement over all datasets except transductive splits, where the underlying KGs are generally larger.
We hypothesize that this gap stems from random walk coverages. Unlike <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Ultra</span> and <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Trix</span> whose message passing guarantees a full <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-hop neighborhood coverage over the queried node, <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Flock</span> relies on sampling random walks, which may not fully cover the target nodes of interest.</p>
</div>
